{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Of the first of your dough you shall set apart a portion as a terumah.</b> The commandment of challah does not apply in the wilderness, but they became obligated in it immediately upon their entry into the land. And the reason it mentioned the expression 'bread of the land' is that this was necessary, because in the wilderness they ate the bread of heaven, which was holy; therefore He commanded that when they come into the land and eat of the bread of the land, they should sanctify it with a commandment, and that is the commandment of challah. And therefore it mentions the Name concerning it, saying: מלחם הארץ תרימו תרומה ל׳ — 'of the bread of the land you shall set apart a terumah to HaShem.' And it is a commandment from the Torah to separate challah from the dough, as it says: ראשית עריסותיכם חלה תרימו תרומה. And the one who separates blesses: אשר קדשנו במצותיו וצונו להפריש חלה — 'Who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to separate challah' — both in the land and outside the land. But the essential commandment of challah from the Torah applies only in the land, as it says: והיה באכלכם מלחם הארץ — 'and it shall be, when you eat of the bread of the land' — and at a time when all Israel are there, as it says: בבאכם — 'when you come' — I said 'in the coming of all of you' and not in the coming of some of you. Therefore challah in this time, even in the days of Ezra in the land of Israel, is only of rabbinic decree. And the reason we separate challah outside the land now is of rabbinic decree, so that the law of challah not be forgotten from Israel. And there is no measure for challah from the Torah: even if he separated as much as a barley-grain, he has exempted the dough. But by rabbinic decree, one separates one part in twenty-four of the dough, so that there be in it a gift for the priest, as it says (Deuteronomy 18): תתן לו — 'you shall give to him' — give him something worthy to be given as a gift. And challah in this time, since the essential obligation is of their words, is forbidden in eating only to a priest from whom impurity issues from his body, such as those with emissions, zavim and zavot, menstruants, women after childbirth, and lepers; but other impure persons through contact with impurities, and even one impure through corpse-contact, are permitted in eating. Therefore one gives it to a minor priest who has not yet had an emission, or to a minor girl who has not yet seen menstrual blood, and it is not necessary to separate both of them for fire." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the commandment of challah, which is a single commandment in the dough, alludes to Kenesset Yisrael, of whom it is written (Song of Songs 6:9): אחת היא יונתי תמתי — 'she is one, my dove, my perfect one' — and she is the tithe and the terumah; for she is called תרומה, as it says: חלה תרימו תרומה, and she is called ראשית, as it says: ראשית עריסותיכם. For she is comprehended within the supernal wisdom, which is also called ראשית, which is the beginning of the beginning, alluded to in the word בראשית." "<b>And if</b> you understand this, you will know the essential reason for why the obligation of the commandment is from the Torah only in the land and at a time when the Shekhinah dwells in the Sanctuary — this is 'in the coming of all of you and not in the coming of some of you.' And perhaps it is for this reason that ten words were fixed in this verse."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:47.268634027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}